  On top of that, [38;5;153mtrack()[39m rejects any [38;5;153mdata[39m key matching [38;5;153m/pass|secret|token|auth|cred/i[39m, drops it, and [38;5;153mconsole.warn[39ms.
  That's belt-and-braces for the next person who adds a form, not the primary defense.

  [38;5;153mdata[39m is shallow and primitives only — nested objects get rejected. Keeps records flat for analytics and stops someone
  passing an entire form object by accident.

  [1mThe open question: usernames are personal data.[0m Writing [38;5;153musername: "alice"[39m into [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and later shipping it to
  an analytics backend means you're storing identifiable data, which carries real obligations (GDPR and friends) and is
  the kind of thing that's very hard to walk back once records exist in a pipeline. For product analytics you usually
  want a stable pseudonymous identifier, not the login name.

  - [1mPseudonymous ID[0m — hash the username to a stable opaque value. Analytics can still count distinct users and follow
    one user's funnel; the records aren't personal data. You lose the ability to look up "what did alice do," which for
    product analytics you generally shouldn't be doing anyway. [3mNote:[0m a plain hash of a username is reversible by
    guessing, so this is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation — it reduces exposure, it doesn't exempt you.
  - [1mRaw username[0m — simplest, and supports per-user debugging. Makes every record personal data, in browser storage you
    don't control.
  - [1mNeither[0m — record only that a login happened, with the outcome. Answers "how many logins succeeded," not "who logged
    in."

  [1mMy recommendation: pseudonymous ID.[0m It gives you the analytics you asked for, and it's the choice that's cheap now and
  expensive later — switching to hashing after raw usernames are in the pipeline means a data cleanup, not a code
  change.

